# Reading binary numbers

Binary numbers use only the digits 0 and 1. Each place is worth twice the place to its right: 1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64 and 128.

{% binary-bits value=14 bits=4 caption="1110 is 8 + 4 + 2 = 14" /%}

To read a binary number, add up the place values that hold a 1. The rightmost place is the 1s place.

{% check question="What is the binary number 100 in decimal?" options=[{id: "a", text: "4"}, {id: "b", text: "100"}, {id: "c", text: "1"}] answer="a" explain="The only 1 is in the 4s place." /%}

# Writing numbers in binary

Start at the largest place value that fits into the number, write 1 there and subtract it. Then go right one place at a time: write 1 when the place value fits into what is left, and 0 when it does not.

{% binary-bits value=22 bits=8 caption="22 = 16 + 4 + 2, so 22 is 10110 in binary" /%}

{% check question="Which binary number is 8?" options=[{id: "a", text: "1000"}, {id: "b", text: "0001"}, {id: "c", text: "111"}] answer="a" explain="8 is a single 1 in the 8s place: 1000." /%}
